/* Original study briefs. Labels come from curriculum.js; these are not transcriptions of reference lessons. */
window.ACADEMY_TOPIC_NOTES = {
 'core-challenges-in-web-scale-app':['Scale exposes coordination costs.','A fast single-node service fails when requests, data and failure domains grow.','Workload growth increases contention, network uncertainty and operational state; treat each as a separate constraint.',['Locate the first saturated resource.','Identify the invariant most at risk.','Test partial failure with realistic load.'],'Adding components can relocate rather than remove a bottleneck.','Why measure tail latency?','Slow outliers can dominate user experience, especially when one response waits for many downstream calls.','Name the bottleneck before selecting a pattern.'],
 'how-to-scale-a-system':['Evolve from observed limits.','One application and database can no longer handle peak traffic.','Scale reads, writes, computation and storage independently according to the limiting resource.',['Measure the current capacity.','Remove unnecessary work.','Add replicas, caching or partitions at the relevant boundary.'],'Distribution adds coordination and failure modes.','When is vertical scaling reasonable?','When a larger node meets the workload and availability needs with less complexity than distribution.','Each scaling step should address measured pressure.'],
 'system-design-interview-study-guide':['Practice reasoning in a repeatable sequence.','Memorizing architectures fails when an interviewer changes a requirement.','Build understanding by tracing one operation, estimating its workload and explaining its recovery.',['Study requirements and communication first.','Practice storage and scaling trade-offs.','Review one timed design and its weak decisions.'],'Breadth without applied recall produces shallow familiarity.','How do you test understanding?','Explain the mechanism without notes, then change one constraint and revise the design.','Practice decisions, not diagrams alone.'],
 'api-design-example':['Make the contract concrete.','An order creation API cannot distinguish a retry from a second purchase.','Model an order resource with stable identity, validated input and observable lifecycle.',['Specify POST /orders and its response.','Use a stable intent key for retries.','Expose GET /orders/{id} for status.'],'An asynchronous operation needs a completion contract.','Does an accepted request imply success?','No. Acceptance and durable business completion are different states.','Design success, conflict and pending responses together.'],
 'api-design-authentication':['Establish who is calling.','An expired or stolen token reaches a protected endpoint.','Authentication verifies a caller or credential; resource permissions are a separate authorization decision.',['Validate signature, issuer and audience.','Check expiry and token purpose.','Protect transport and avoid logging credentials.'],'Self-contained tokens reduce lookup work but complicate immediate revocation.','Is decoding a JWT sufficient?','No. Decoding does not verify its signature, claims or trust context.','A credential must be verified, not merely parsed.'],
 'api-design-authorization':['Check permission at the resource boundary.','A user changes an account ID in a URL and reads another tenant’s data.','Authorize the actor, action and target resource using trusted identity and tenant context.',['Deny by default.','Scope queries to authorized resources.','Test cross-tenant and object-level access.'],'Cached permissions can become stale after policy changes.','Does login imply resource access?','No. A valid identity may still lack permission for this operation and object.','Authentication does not replace authorization.'],
 'api-gateway':['Centralize edge policies carefully.','Every service implements inconsistent routing and request limits.','A gateway applies shared edge behavior before forwarding to services that retain business-level checks.',['Route using explicit rules.','Apply authentication and admission limits.','Propagate request identity and deadlines.'],'A gateway adds a shared dependency and an extra hop.','Can services trust arbitrary tenant headers?','No. Trusted identity context must come from an authenticated boundary, not user-controlled forwarding headers.','Keep business authorization close to the data.'],
 'system-design-components':['Choose components by responsibility.','A diagram lists products but does not explain ownership.','Compute, storage, caches, queues and routing solve different problems with different durability boundaries.',['Assign a source of truth.','Label interaction protocols.','State each component’s failure behavior.'],'Every additional component increases the operating surface.','What justifies adding a queue?','A need for asynchronous execution, buffering or independent consumption together with a completion and recovery contract.','Connect every component to a requirement.'],
 'availability':['Define availability from the user’s perspective.','Servers are running while checkout requests fail.','Availability measures successful eligible operations over a defined observation window and boundary.',['Choose a request-based or time-based SLI.','Define good responses.','Budget errors across dependencies.'],'A component uptime figure may hide business failures.','Is a 200 response always available service?','Not if the business operation is incorrect or violates the success definition.','Measure outcomes rather than process existence.'],
 'how-to-achieve-high-availability':['Remove correlated failure paths.','Two replicas fail together because they share the same power, zone or configuration fault.','Redundancy helps only when failure domains, routing and recovery are designed together.',['Separate failure domains.','Test detection and failover.','Maintain spare capacity for a failed node or zone.'],'Automated failover can amplify a false health signal.','Why test failover under load?','The surviving capacity and dependency limits may be insufficient even when routing changes correctly.','Redundancy needs a working recovery path.'],
 'tech-stacks-to-achieve-high-availability':['Redundancy is a system property.','A highly available application depends on a single database or identity service.','Evaluate availability across the entire request path, including managed infrastructure and operational controls.',['Use multiple application instances and health-based routing.','Select database replication and promotion policies.','Rehearse regional dependencies and restores.'],'Managed services still have configuration and shared-failure risks.','Does multi-zone deployment eliminate downtime?','No. Application defects, bad releases, shared dependencies and incorrect failover can affect every zone.','Evaluate the weakest critical dependency.'],
 'latency':['Measure how long one operation takes.','Average latency looks healthy while a small fraction of users wait seconds.','Latency includes queueing, execution and downstream waits; percentiles describe its distribution.',['Measure the user-visible boundary.','Inspect p50, p95 and p99.','Separate queue time from service time.'],'Aggressive timeouts may convert slow requests into retries.','What does p99 describe?','Approximately 99 percent of measured requests completed within that duration for the stated sample and window.','Keep the percentile and measurement boundary together.'],
 'throughput':['Count completed work per unit time.','Adding concurrent requests increases queueing without increasing completions.','Throughput is bounded by the slowest constrained stage, not by the number of callers.',['Measure completions per second.','Identify saturation and queue growth.','Load-test with representative request costs.'],'High throughput can coexist with unacceptable latency.','Are concurrency and throughput the same?','No. Concurrency counts work in progress; throughput counts work completed over time.','Use throughput together with latency and resource use.'],
 'qps':['Translate traffic into rate and concurrency.','Daily volume hides a sharp peak that exhausts connections.','QPS is a rate; in a stable system average work in progress is approximately arrival rate times average time in system.',['Convert daily requests to average QPS.','Apply a justified peak factor.','Estimate concurrency with consistent units.'],'Averages hide bursts and heavy-tailed request costs.','At 200 QPS and 0.1 seconds average response time, what is average concurrency?','About 20 in-flight requests under stable conditions and the same measurement boundary.','Rates require a time window and workload assumptions.'],
 'back-of-envelope-calculation-real-world-examples':['Size a concrete workload.','A photo service needs storage, bandwidth and request-capacity estimates.','Use independent estimates for uploads, downloads, retained bytes and peak processing.',['For 1M uploads/day at 2 MB, raw growth is 2 TB/day.','Estimate replication and retention separately.','Calculate download bandwidth from size and rate.'],'Compression, metadata and skew change real capacity.','Does raw size include replicas?','No. Replication, indexes, metadata and operational headroom must be accounted for separately.','Keep decimal and binary units explicit.'],
 'microservices':['A boundary trades local simplicity for independent change.','One deployment couples teams, but splitting it introduces network failures and distributed state.','A microservice owns a business capability and its contract; a modular monolith can preserve boundaries in one process.',['Start with domain ownership.','Identify independent release or scaling needs.','Avoid shared writes across service-owned data.'],'Fine-grained services can create chatty, fragile call chains.','When is a monolith preferable?','When independent scaling and deployment do not justify the coordination and operating costs of separate services.','Service boundaries should reflect ownership.'],
 'microservice-communications':['Select the interaction contract first.','A service cannot tell whether a downstream operation finished after a connection failure.','Calls, events and commands have different coupling, ordering and completion semantics.',['Choose whether the caller must wait.','Define identities and schemas.','Document duplicates, timeouts and recovery.'],'Asynchronous transport removes immediate waiting but adds workflow state.','Is an event the same as a command?','A command requests an action; an event records a fact that has happened.','Match the message type to the business meaning.'],
 'synchronous-communication':['The caller waits for an answer.','A checkout request blocks on inventory and payment dependencies.','Synchronous calls couple response latency and availability to downstream behavior.',['Set a total deadline.','Bound connection and execution waits.','Limit concurrency per dependency.'],'A chain of individually fast calls can have a slow combined tail.','What if one dependency stalls?','Without bounded waits and concurrency, stalled calls can exhaust the caller’s resources.','Waiting is a resource allocation decision.'],
 'synchronous-communication-implementation':['Make the client boundary explicit.','An HTTP client opens unlimited connections and waits indefinitely.','A production client needs bounded pools, deadlines, schema validation and observable error classification.',['Configure connect and request limits.','Reuse connections with bounded pools.','Propagate trace IDs and remaining deadlines.'],'Client defaults may not fit your failure budget.','Should a retry create a new business identity?','No. Retry attempts belong to the original intent and must preserve its identity.','Client configuration is part of correctness.'],
 'synchronous-communication-failure-handling':['Classify failures before recovery.','Validation errors, overload and unknown payment outcomes are all retried identically.','Recovery depends on whether work was rejected, definitively failed or may already have committed.',['Separate terminal and transient errors.','Keep ambiguous side effects unresolved.','Apply bounded retries and isolation.'],'Retries can amplify overload or duplicate unprotected effects.','Why distinguish timeout from confirmed decline?','A decline is an authoritative result; a timeout leaves the remote outcome unknown.','Choose recovery from evidence, not just exception type.'],
 'fallbacks':['Degrade service without inventing correctness.','A pricing service fails and checkout considers substituting a cached or default price.','A fallback is a documented alternate behavior with its own freshness and correctness limits.',['Identify safe degraded functionality.','Label stale data where meaningful.','Fail closed for operations that require authoritative state.'],'A fallback can hide an incident or create incorrect decisions.','Is cached inventory safe for a final sale decision?','Only if the business contract tolerates its staleness; authoritative reservation usually needs stronger coordination.','A degraded response must preserve the critical invariant.'],
 'service-discovery':['Locate healthy service instances.','An instance moves and clients keep calling its old address.','Discovery maps a stable service identity to changing endpoints through DNS, a registry or a platform proxy.',['Choose a discovery authority.','Set readiness and removal behavior.','Handle cached endpoints and connection draining.'],'Aggressive endpoint caching delays reaction to membership changes.','Does discovery guarantee an endpoint stays healthy?','No. A returned endpoint can fail immediately; calls still need deadlines and failure handling.','Discovery supplies candidates, not a success guarantee.'],
 'microservice-async-comm':['Separate acceptance from eventual execution.','A user action must continue while a downstream worker is unavailable.','Messaging transfers work to a durable channel and independent consumer, with explicit completion tracking.',['Persist the intent before acknowledging acceptance.','Assign stable message identity.','Define retries and terminal failure handling.'],'Eventual execution adds latency and unresolved-work ownership.','What does a broker acknowledgement establish?','It establishes the broker’s documented acceptance guarantee, not completion of the consumer’s business effect.','Track delivery and business completion separately.'],
 'message-queue-use-cases':['Choose a queue for a concrete coordination need.','Uploads arrive faster than workers can process them during short bursts.','Queues buffer work, distribute tasks and decouple producer lifetimes from consumer execution.',['Estimate arrival and drain rates.','Separate independent consumers where required.','Set backlog-age and dead-letter alerts.'],'A queue cannot absorb a permanent capacity deficit.','When will a backlog never drain?','When sustained processing capacity is no greater than the incoming work rate.','A backlog is a measurable recovery obligation.'],
 'redis-queue-tutorial':['Practice durable ownership with Redis Streams.','A worker reads a job and dies before recording completion.','Redis Streams consumer groups track pending deliveries; acknowledgement follows a protected business effect.',['Create a stream and consumer group.','Read with XREADGROUP and acknowledge with XACK after commit.','Recover abandoned pending work and deduplicate effects.'],'Persistence and failover settings determine loss risks; Redis is not automatically durable.','Why inspect pending entries?','They identify delivered but unacknowledged work that may need recovery after consumer failure.','Test the crash between effect and acknowledgement.'],
 'log-based-message-queues':['Retain history independently of consumers.','A second consumer needs to replay events that the first has already processed.','An append-only log keeps records for a retention policy while consumers maintain their own positions.',['Partition around ordering needs.','Track offsets per consumer group.','Plan retention and replay capacity.'],'Slow consumers can fall behind retained history.','Does consuming a log record delete it?','Typically not; retention is managed separately from each consumer’s progress.','Separate log lifetime from consumer progress.'],
 'kafka-tutorial':['Observe partitions, groups and replay.','Two workers must process a keyed event stream and recover from a crash.','A Kafka exercise should demonstrate the partition boundary and the difference between processing and offset commit.',['Produce events keyed by account ID.','Run two consumers in one group.','Crash after effect but before commit and inspect replay.'],'A local lab does not establish production durability or capacity.','What should a replay-safe consumer preserve?','One intended effect per stable operation identity despite repeated delivery.','Make the failure window visible in the lab.'],
 'evolution-of-computing-environments':['Infrastructure changes the unit you operate.','A team moves from physical servers to containers but still needs capacity and isolation.','VMs virtualize machines; containers isolate processes while sharing a kernel; managed execution shifts some operational duties.',['Compare startup, isolation and packing needs.','Identify what the platform manages.','Plan persistent data and failure boundaries.'],'Abstraction reduces some administration but does not remove application limits.','Does a container provide a durable database volume by itself?','No. Persistent storage needs an explicit lifecycle and durability arrangement.','Understand what remains your responsibility.'],
 'evolution-of-a-web-app-stateless-vs-stateful':['Separate disposable compute from required state.','Users lose carts when traffic moves to another application instance.','A stateless request handler can serve any request using external or self-contained state; it still participates in a stateful system.',['Identify session and workflow state.','Move durable state to an appropriate owner.','Test restart and cross-instance routing.'],'External state adds network and store dependencies.','Are stateless services free of state?','No. They avoid depending on unshared in-process state across requests.','Make instance replacement safe.'],
 'load-balancing-code-lab':['Demonstrate routing during failure.','One backend is killed while clients continuously send requests.','A load-balancing lab should expose distribution, health checks and in-flight request behavior.',['Run two identifiable backends behind a proxy.','Send requests and observe distribution.','Stop one backend and measure failure and recovery time.'],'Health-check intervals trade detection speed against probe load and false positives.','Can requests fail during removal?','Yes. Detection and routing changes are not instantaneous, and existing connections may still break.','Measure the transition, not only the steady state.'],
 'auto-scaling':['Capacity arrives after the scaling signal.','A sudden burst exceeds capacity before new instances are ready.','Autoscaling adjusts replicas from observed demand, with delays from measurement, scheduling and warm-up.',['Choose a workload-related signal.','Maintain minimum failure headroom.','Test scale-out latency and scale-in draining.'],'CPU alone can miss queueing or downstream saturation.','Why can scaling out overload a database?','Each new application instance may add its own connection pool and increase concurrent database work.','Scale with dependency limits in view.'],
 'read-write-separation':['Route reads with an explicit freshness policy.','Read traffic competes with writes on the leader.','Replicas can serve eligible reads while the leader handles writes; replication lag changes what readers observe.',['Classify freshness-sensitive reads.','Route read-after-write operations appropriately.','Monitor replica lag and failover behavior.'],'Replicas help reads but do not inherently increase single-leader write capacity.','Can a replica immediately show a just-committed write?','Only under a contract that establishes the relevant replication position or equivalent freshness guarantee.','Offload reads without hiding staleness.'],
 'cqrs':['Separate command intent from read shape.','A transaction model is efficient for writes but expensive for dashboards.','CQRS uses distinct command and query models; they may share a database or use separate projections.',['Keep invariants in the command model.','Build projections for actual queries.','Expose projection lag and replay behavior.'],'Separate models add synchronization and operational work.','Does CQRS require event sourcing?','No. Command/query separation and event sourcing are independent architectural choices.','Separate models only when the benefit pays for coordination.'],
 'caching-tiers':['Each cache layer owns a freshness boundary.','A value is refreshed in Redis but remains stale in browser and CDN caches.','Browser, edge, process and distributed caches reduce different costs and can expire independently.',['Map all copies of a response.','Define cacheability at each layer.','Trace invalidation from source to consumer.'],'More layers improve locality but complicate freshness diagnosis.','Does clearing Redis clear browser caches?','No. Each layer needs its own expiration or invalidation mechanism.','Document the whole cache path.'],
 'cdn-vs-app-cache':['Cache near the cost you want to avoid.','Global users fetch identical assets from one region, while personalized responses require application context.','A CDN caches eligible HTTP responses at the edge; application caches reuse data or computations inside the service boundary.',['Separate public and private responses.','Define HTTP cache-control and variation.','Measure origin offload and regional latency.'],'Incorrect shared caching can disclose private data.','Can all authenticated responses share one CDN key?','No. Eligibility and key variation must preserve the authorization and personalization boundary.','Location and cache key jointly determine safety.'],
 'cache-key-design':['A key defines which requests may share a result.','Two tenants request the same object ID and receive one cached response.','Keys must represent the full result identity: tenant, object, relevant query parameters and representation version.',['Include trusted tenant scope.','Canonicalize relevant inputs.','Version keys for incompatible result changes.'],'Overly detailed keys reduce reuse; incomplete keys mix distinct results.','Should secrets appear directly in cache keys?','Avoid them; keys often appear in logs and diagnostics. Use non-sensitive scoped identifiers.','A cache key is a correctness boundary.'],
 'caching-read-patterns':['Choose who owns loading on a miss.','A cache misses and many application threads independently load the same expensive row.','Cache-aside loads through the application; read-through delegates loading to a cache abstraction. Both need miss and outage policies.',['Define the authoritative loader.','Coalesce concurrent loads.','Decide whether to cache absent results briefly.'],'Negative caching can delay visibility of newly created data.','Why coalesce cache misses?','It reduces duplicated source work when many requests miss the same key simultaneously.','Optimize the miss path before the hit path.'],
 'cache-lab-read':['Measure hits, misses and source work.','A cache reports a good hit rate while a few expensive misses dominate load.','A read drill compares latency and source-call counts with cold, warm and skewed traffic.',['Send repeated reads for a hot key.','Expire it while concurrent readers arrive.','Compare source calls with and without request coalescing.'],'A synthetic uniform workload can hide hot-key behavior.','What should improve beyond hit rate?','Source work, user latency and resilience during cold starts must improve too.','Count the work saved.'],
 'caching-write-patterns':['A write policy chooses when copies converge.','A database update succeeds but a cached result still holds the old value.','Write-through coordinates cache updates in the write path; write-behind delays persistence; cache-aside often invalidates after source writes.',['Identify the authoritative store.','Specify update ordering.','Test crashes between each step.'],'Write-behind requires durable buffering if accepted writes must survive cache loss.','Does writing two systems sequentially make them atomic?','No. A crash between writes can leave them inconsistent.','Specify every intermediate state.'],
 'cache-lab-write':['Reproduce stale-fill races.','A slow reader loads an old row while a writer commits a newer version.','Control read and write timing to test whether an old loader can refill the cache after invalidation.',['Pause a reader after source fetch.','Commit an update and invalidate.','Resume the reader and inspect cached version.'],'Sequential tests miss concurrency bugs.','What assertion detects the race?','The cache must satisfy the documented freshness policy despite a delayed old loader.','Test interleavings deliberately.'],
 'cache-invalidation':['Freshness needs a time and version contract.','An update reaches the source but clients keep reading an old cached value.','TTL, explicit invalidation and versioned keys address different freshness and coordination requirements.',['Define acceptable staleness.','Deliver invalidations reliably where needed.','Measure delayed loaders and invalidation lag.'],'TTL limits cache lifetime after insertion, not necessarily age since source mutation.','Can one TTL describe every freshness delay?','No. Source replica lag, delayed fills and downstream caches can add other delays.','Measure freshness end to end.'],
 'cache-eviction-sizing':['Memory is bounded; choose what leaves.','A cache evicts valuable hot records while large rarely used values consume space.','Capacity includes key bytes, values, metadata and replication overhead; eviction selects victims when limits are reached.',['Estimate working-set size.','Compare LRU/LFU-style behavior against workload.','Track evictions, misses and memory fragmentation.'],'Eviction can cause sudden source-load increases.','Is TTL the same as eviction?','No. TTL expires entries by age; eviction reclaims space according to a capacity policy.','Size the working set and the miss capacity.'],
 'distributed-caching':['Distribution adds placement and coordination.','A cache no longer fits on one node, and membership changes remap keys.','Sharded caches distribute capacity; clients or proxies route keys and handle node failure and remapping.',['Define hashing and membership.','Plan hot-key mitigation.','Test rebalance misses and source protection.'],'Sharding does not split the load of one hot key automatically.','What does consistent hashing reduce?','It reduces the fraction of keys reassigned under typical membership changes; it does not guarantee even traffic.','Balance traffic as well as storage.'],
 'cache-high-availability':['A cache outage must have a bounded consequence.','The primary cache fails and all callers fall through to a fragile database.','Replication or failover can preserve some cache service, but source protection is still required.',['Define degraded behavior.','Reserve source capacity or shed load.','Test promotion and partial cache loss.'],'Replicas may lag and failover may lose cached values.','Does cache replication remove the need for miss handling?','No. Entries can still be absent, evicted or lost during recovery.','Make cache failure survivable.'],
 'cache-cold-start':['Warm-up is a workload spike.','A deployment replaces all process caches and database traffic surges.','Cold caches convert expected hits into misses until the working set is rebuilt.',['Roll out gradually.','Warm selected high-value keys.','Bound concurrent loads and jitter expirations.'],'Prewarming too much data can create the same overload it aims to avoid.','Should every key be preloaded?','Usually not. Prioritize costly, frequently accessed data and measure warm-up load.','Treat warm-up as production traffic.'],
 'cache-failure-modes':['Distinguish absence, slowness and corruption.','A slow cache consumes more time than a direct database read.','Cache timeouts, stampedes, hot keys and stale values require different mitigations.',['Set a cache latency budget.','Protect the source during bypass.','Detect stale or incorrectly scoped entries.'],'Fail-open behavior can overwhelm the source; fail-closed behavior reduces availability.','Is bypassing a failed cache always safe?','Only if downstream capacity and correctness permit the resulting miss load.','The fallback path needs a capacity plan.'],
 'cache-lab-disasters':['Inject failure and observe recovery.','Cache restart, node loss and simultaneous expiry cause unpredictable load.','A disaster drill validates source protection and recovery under controlled failure.',['Record baseline source QPS and latency.','Remove cache capacity or expire hot keys.','Verify load shedding and recovery-time objectives.'],'A drill needs bounded blast radius and representative traffic.','What is a useful success criterion?','User and source-system limits remain within the documented degraded-service contract.','Test failure transitions, not only normal hits.'],
 'cache-security-observability':['Correctness includes isolation and evidence.','A shared cache leaks another tenant’s value and logs expose sensitive keys.','Authorization-aware keys, access controls and redacted diagnostics protect cached data; metrics reveal behavior.',['Scope keys and cache access.','Track hits, latency, evictions and source fallback.','Alert on freshness violations and tenant isolation failures.'],'High hit rate can coexist with wrong or unauthorized results.','What should a cache dashboard include beyond hit rate?','Tail latency, errors, evictions, source load and freshness or isolation signals.','Measure whether cached answers are safe and useful.'],
 'cache-interview-checklist':['Explain the cache contract in order.','An answer says “add Redis” without identifying data, keys or recovery.','A defensible caching design connects the costly read to key identity, freshness, capacity and failure behavior.',['Name cached data and source.','Choose read/write and eviction policies.','Walk a miss, concurrent update and outage.'],'Caching unnecessary work can conceal the original database design problem.','What makes the cache justified?','Measured repeatable work and an acceptable staleness contract with a viable failure path.','Make every cached copy accountable.'],
 'dataflow-system-design':['Follow one fact from creation to every consumer.','Services disagree because nobody knows which copy is authoritative.','A dataflow describes producers, transformations, stores and readers together with ownership and ordering.',['Mark the source of each fact.','Label synchronous and asynchronous edges.','Trace retries and stale copies.'],'Unlabeled arrows conceal failure and latency assumptions.','What should an event edge specify?','Identity, schema, ordering scope, delivery behavior and consumer recovery.','A useful arrow carries a contract.'],
 'push-vs-pull':['Decide who initiates work.','Consumers either poll repeatedly or receive updates they cannot process fast enough.','Push sends updates when available; pull lets consumers request work according to their pace.',['Compare freshness requirements.','Plan backpressure and retries.','Measure wasted polls or delivery fan-out.'],'Push improves immediacy but needs overload controls; pull can add delay and polling cost.','Can push operate without buffering?','Only if loss or rejection is acceptable; durable delivery usually needs retained state and recovery.','Match initiation to demand and capacity.'],
 'push-vs-pull-twitter-timeline':['Place feed assembly on writes or reads.','A celebrity post would require millions of per-follower updates.','Fan-out-on-write precomputes feeds; fan-out-on-read assembles posts when the user asks. A hybrid can treat high-fan-out authors differently.',['Estimate follower and read skew.','Choose write versus read amplification.','Define ordering and deletion propagation.'],'A hybrid reduces hotspots but complicates merge and freshness behavior.','Why not push every post to every follower?','Extreme fan-out can make one write disproportionately expensive and slow.','Choose the cost location using workload skew.'],
 'data-structures-behind-databases':['Storage engines make workload trade-offs.','A structure optimized for point lookups performs poorly on ranges or sustained writes.','Hash tables, trees and sorted runs organize data differently and shift read, write and space costs.',['List point, range and update patterns.','Identify memory and disk access costs.','Compare maintenance and compaction work.'],'Logical query convenience does not remove physical I/O costs.','Why are sorted structures useful?','They support ordered traversal and range access without scanning every key.','Connect query behavior to physical organization.'],
 'b-tree':['Keep ordered search shallow.','A disk-resident index needs efficient point and range queries.','B-tree families maintain balanced, multi-key pages; many database indexes use B+ tree variants with linked leaves.',['Search page separators.','Read the relevant leaf range.','Account for splits and concurrent updates.'],'Random updates and extra indexes add write and cache costs.','Why use high fan-out?','More keys per page reduce tree height and page reads for large datasets.','Ordered pages enable efficient bounded scans.'],
 'sstable':['Write sorted immutable runs.','A storage engine must persist sorted key/value data for later search and merge.','An SSTable is an immutable sorted file typically paired with block indexes and optional Bloom filters.',['Sort records before writing.','Use indexes to locate blocks.','Merge runs and discard obsolete versions when safe.'],'Overlapping runs can require multiple reads.','Can a Bloom filter prove a key exists?','No. It can indicate possible membership or definite absence under its normal assumptions.','Sorted immutability makes merging practical.'],
 'lsm-tree':['Buffer writes, then merge sorted history.','Random disk updates limit write throughput.','An LSM design buffers writes in memory, flushes sorted runs and compacts them over time; durability commonly uses a write-ahead log.',['Track memory, log and flush behavior.','Tune compaction for workload.','Monitor write, read and space amplification.'],'Compaction consumes I/O and can create latency spikes.','Are acknowledged writes safe solely because they entered memory?','No. The acknowledgement policy must establish the required durable log or replication guarantee.','Fast ingestion needs a sustainable compaction budget.'],
 'introduction-to-storage':['Store facts under an explicit durability contract.','An application acknowledges a write that disappears after restart.','Storage selection includes data model, access methods, persistence, recovery and operational lifecycle.',['Define what acknowledgement means.','Specify backup and restore objectives.','Test the dominant queries and failure modes.'],'Durability, latency and operating complexity constrain each other.','What distinguishes durability from availability?','Durability concerns preserving accepted data; availability concerns serving eligible operations.','Define persistence before optimizing access.'],
 'sql-database':['Use relations and constraints to protect shared facts.','Two concurrent orders claim the same unique business identity.','Relational databases provide structured queries, constraints and transactions with product-specific isolation behavior.',['Model keys and relationships.','Put uniqueness in constraints.','Inspect plans and concurrency anomalies.'],'Indexes and strict coordination add write costs and contention.','Does a transaction guarantee every invariant automatically?','No. Statement shape, constraints and isolation determine which races remain possible.','Put invariants at the database boundary.'],
 'nosql-database':['Choose a model, not a category slogan.','A team assumes all non-relational databases have the same scaling and consistency properties.','Key/value, document, wide-column and graph systems optimize different access patterns and expose different guarantees.',['Identify query and update shapes.','Check transaction and consistency scope.','Prototype partition-key behavior.'],'A flexible model can shift validation and join work into applications.','Does NoSQL mean no transactions?','No. Capabilities depend on the product, operation and configuration.','Evaluate the actual contract.'],
 'key-value-database':['Make the key do the locating.','A workload mostly reads or replaces a complete value by identifier.','A key/value store uses an explicit key for access; secondary queries and cross-key invariants need additional support.',['Choose stable scoped keys.','Bound value size.','Define atomic updates and expiration behavior.'],'Poor keys create hotspots or make required queries expensive.','Can arbitrary attribute queries be assumed?','No. Secondary indexing and query capabilities vary by implementation.','Design keys from access patterns.'],
 'document-database':['Model the aggregate you read and update.','An order is commonly loaded with its line items and shipping details.','Documents group related fields and nested structures; embedding versus references controls locality and update boundaries.',['Identify aggregate boundaries.','Check growth and document-size limits.','Index actual filters and sort orders.'],'Embedding can duplicate data and make unbounded arrays problematic.','Does schema flexibility mean validation is unnecessary?','No. Applications and database validation still need to enforce business structure and invariants.','Bound aggregates and validate their shape.'],
 'full-text-search-database':['Index language for retrieval.','A substring scan over millions of articles is slow and returns poor relevance.','An inverted index maps analyzed terms to documents, enabling token-based retrieval and ranking.',['Choose analyzers and tokenization.','Design relevance and filters.','Plan indexing lag, updates and deletion.'],'Search indexes are often derived copies with freshness delays.','Should search results bypass authorization?','No. Filtering and final resource access must respect the caller’s permissions.','Relevance and access control are separate requirements.'],
 'oltp-vs-olap':['Separate transactional work from large scans.','A dashboard scan competes with checkout updates.','OLTP targets short operational transactions; OLAP targets analytical scans, aggregations and historical comparisons.',['Classify query size and latency.','Choose row or column-oriented access appropriately.','Define replication or pipeline freshness.'],'A separate analytics system adds ingestion lag and reconciliation work.','Can one database serve both workloads?','Sometimes, but resource isolation and workload scale determine whether the compromise is acceptable.','Match physical storage and isolation to workload.'],
 'blob-object-storage':['Store large immutable objects with metadata.','Application disks fill with uploaded videos and become a recovery liability.','Object storage addresses blobs by key and separates object lifecycle from application-instance lifetime.',['Use scoped object keys and access policies.','Upload large objects using supported multipart flows.','Define checksums, retention and deletion behavior.'],'Object APIs do not imply filesystem rename or transaction semantics.','Should the application trust an uploaded filename as a storage path?','No. Generate controlled keys and validate access, content limits and ownership.','Separate blob bytes from transactional metadata.'],
 'database-scaling':['Identify which storage operation is constrained.','A database has spare disk capacity but cannot keep up with writes.','Query optimization, larger nodes, read replicas and partitioning address different limits.',['Inspect query plans, locks and I/O.','Reduce unnecessary work.','Scale the constrained read or write path.'],'Sharding increases coordination and operating complexity.','Will a read replica fix leader write contention?','Not directly. It can offload eligible reads, but writes still encounter the leader’s coordination limit.','Match the scaling mechanism to the constraint.'],
 'implementing-database-replication':['Make promotion and fencing explicit.','A network partition leaves two nodes believing they can accept writes.','Replication recovery needs leader selection, log-position reasoning and a mechanism that prevents stale leaders from writing.',['Define acknowledgement and promotion criteria.','Fence the old writer.','Verify client routing and divergent history handling.'],'Failover may trade recovery speed against data-loss risk.','Why is promotion alone insufficient?','The old leader may still be alive; without fencing it can accept conflicting writes.','Failover includes stopping the former authority.'],
 'database-replication-codelab':['Observe lag and promotion rather than assuming them.','A local replica reads old data immediately after a leader commit.','A replication lab tracks a write’s durable position, apply progress and read visibility.',['Write a known sequence.','Pause replication and inspect stale reads.','Resume and verify convergence before testing controlled failover.'],'A small lab does not establish production loss or recovery guarantees.','What proves the replica caught up?','Evidence that it applied at least the relevant committed position, not merely that it is reachable.','Measure progress through the log.'],
 'change-data-capture':['Publish committed changes from storage history.','A search index needs database changes without a fragile application dual write.','CDC reads a change log or equivalent committed change feed and delivers records to downstream consumers.',['Persist source offsets.','Handle initial snapshot and live-log handoff.','Apply changes idempotently and monitor lag.'],'Schema changes and expired log history can break recovery.','Does CDC automatically create exactly-once sink effects?','No. Retries and sink transactions still need replay-safe handling.','Preserve source position and operation identity.'],
 'advanced-database-partitioning':['Choose keys for skew, locality and change.','A tenant-based shard becomes overloaded as one tenant grows far beyond the rest.','Composite keys, buckets, directory routing and tenant isolation can balance traffic while preserving selected locality.',['Estimate tenant and key skew.','Model cross-partition queries.','Plan split, merge and migration protocols.'],'Spreading one tenant may make its reads and transactions more expensive.','Why not use a random key for every workload?','Random placement balances some writes but can scatter related reads and multi-record invariants.','Partition choice is a query and operations trade-off.'],
 'consistent-hashing':['Limit remapping as membership changes.','Adding one node to modulo-based placement moves many keys.','A hash ring assigns key ranges to members; virtual nodes can improve ownership balance under normal assumptions.',['Hash keys and node positions.','Route to the owning range.','Version membership and plan data movement.'],'A ring alone does not replicate data or solve hot-key load.','Does consistent hashing guarantee balanced requests?','No. Key popularity and value size can remain highly skewed.','Placement stability is only one part of distribution.'],
 'database-partition-codelab':['Exercise routing and movement safely.','A key is read from its old shard while migration is in progress.','A partition lab should validate routing ownership, copy/catch-up and cutover under concurrent writes.',['Create two shards and deterministic routing.','Move a bounded key range.','Verify counts, values and stale-writer rejection.'],'A copy without catch-up can lose changes made during migration.','What must cutover establish?','A single authoritative writer and a destination that has caught up to the required boundary.','Migration needs both data convergence and writer ownership.'],
 'unix-pipelines':['Compose small transformations.','A large log file needs filtering, projection and aggregation.','Pipes stream one process’s output into another, while individual stages may still buffer or materialize data.',['Filter records early.','Normalize fields before grouping.','Check exit status and malformed input.'],'Sorting and high-cardinality aggregation can consume disk or memory.','Is every pipeline constant-memory?','No. Operations such as sorting require retained state or external spill.','Know which stages stream and which accumulate.'],
 'mapreduce-model':['Partition work around a shuffle boundary.','A dataset is too large for one machine’s grouped aggregation.','Map emits key/value pairs; shuffle groups keys; reduce combines each group.',['Define deterministic mapping.','Measure key skew and shuffle volume.','Make retried output commits safe.'],'Shuffle can dominate network and disk costs.','Why can one key slow the whole job?','Its group may send disproportionate work to one reducer, creating a straggler.','Balance groups, not just input splits.'],
 'distributed-file-systems':['Separate file metadata from replicated blocks.','A dataset must survive worker failure while supporting parallel scans.','Distributed file systems place blocks across nodes and maintain metadata that maps files to block locations.',['Choose block and replication policies.','Plan metadata availability.','Verify checksums, repair and locality.'],'Replication consumes space and bandwidth; correlated failure remains possible.','Does replication replace backup?','No. Logical deletion or corruption can propagate to replicas; independent recoverable history is still needed.','Availability of bytes and recovery of history are different goals.'],
 'modern-batch-spark':['Plan execution as stages and partitions.','A transformation looks parallel but a shuffle and skewed join dominate runtime.','Spark builds an execution plan whose partitioning, shuffles, memory use and retries determine performance.',['Inspect the execution plan.','Reduce shuffle and handle skew.','Use deterministic transformations and safe output commits.'],'Caching intermediate data consumes memory and can worsen pressure.','Can a retried task repeat an external API call?','Yes. Task retry semantics do not protect unrelated external side effects.','Keep retryable computation separate from unprotected effects.'],
 'stream-processing-intro':['Maintain results while data keeps arriving.','A fraud signal must update without waiting for a daily batch.','Stream processing continuously transforms events and often maintains keyed state with checkpoints and replay.',['Define event identity and ordering scope.','Set state retention and progress rules.','Test restart and backpressure.'],'Unbounded input requires bounded state and an explicit late-data policy.','Does processing immediately guarantee complete results?','No. Events can arrive late, out of order or not at all.','Freshness and completeness need separate contracts.'],
 'windowing-patterns':['Bound aggregation with explicit time membership.','A stream needs counts per minute rather than one count that grows forever.','Tumbling windows are non-overlapping; sliding windows overlap; session windows group activity separated by inactivity gaps.',['Choose event or processing time.','Specify window boundaries and triggers.','Define late updates and cleanup.'],'Overlapping windows increase state and repeated aggregation work.','Can a late event change a session boundary?','Yes. Depending on the policy it may bridge previously separate activity and require corrected output.','Window shape determines result meaning.'],
 'delivery-guarantees':['State the effect boundary precisely.','A worker commits output and crashes before acknowledging its input.','At-most-once can lose work; at-least-once can repeat work; exactly-once claims require a defined coordination boundary.',['Locate the acknowledgement point.','Coordinate state, output and progress where supported.','Deduplicate external effects independently.'],'A platform guarantee may not cover an external database or provider.','Does exactly-once delivery imply one bank charge?','No. The charge needs its own transactional or idempotency contract within the relevant effect boundary.','Name the covered input, state and output.'],
 'modern-stream-flink':['Compare processing models and operational fit.','A team must choose an engine for keyed joins and stateful event-time processing.','Flink and Kafka Streams provide stateful stream processing with different deployment, integration and management models.',['Compare event-time and state requirements.','Check checkpoint and recovery behavior.','Prototype joins, skew and external sinks.'],'Engine features do not automatically establish end-to-end business guarantees.','What must be verified for an external sink?','Whether retries, transactions or deduplication preserve the intended effect when the processor restarts.','Evaluate recovery at the sink boundary.'],
 'lambda-architecture':['Combine recomputation with a low-latency path.','Fast provisional results need correction from a complete historical dataset.','Lambda architecture maintains batch and speed paths whose outputs are combined for serving.',['Retain durable raw input.','Define reconciliation of batch and speed results.','Keep the two computations semantically aligned.'],'Duplicated processing logic can diverge and increase operating cost.','What happens when the two paths disagree?','The design needs an explicit precedence and correction policy rather than silently merging incompatible results.','Two paths require one shared definition of correctness.'],
 'kappa-architecture':['Use replay through a stream path.','Maintaining separate batch and streaming implementations causes drift.','Kappa-style designs use a stream-processing path for live work and historical replay when retained input permits it.',['Retain sufficient event history.','Version processing and output.','Replay into isolated state before cutover.'],'Large replays compete for resources and require compatible historical schemas.','Can a short-retention log rebuild years of history?','No. Required input must remain available somewhere for a complete rebuild.','Replayability depends on retained evidence.'],
 'batch-stream-unification':['Share semantics across bounded and unbounded input.','Daily recomputation and live processing implement subtly different business rules.','Unified processing aims to express common logic while accounting for windows, event time and boundedness.',['Define common transformation semantics.','Specify completion for bounded input.','Test equivalent datasets under both execution modes.'],'A shared API does not make runtime costs or timing behavior identical.','What still differs between batch and live input?','Input completeness, progress detection, state lifetime and when outputs can be considered final.','Unify meaning before execution machinery.'],
 'etl-vs-elt':['Choose where transformation runs.','Raw events must be validated and transformed before analytical use.','ETL transforms before loading the target; ELT loads first and transforms using target-side capabilities.',['Classify sensitive or invalid data.','Choose transformation and retention boundaries.','Track lineage and reproducible versions.'],'Loading raw data increases governance obligations; early transformation can discard useful history.','Does ELT remove data quality work?','No. Validation, deduplication, access control and correction remain necessary.','Transformation location is a resource and governance choice.'],
 'backfill-reprocessing':['Replay history without corrupting live results.','A bug fix requires rebuilding six months of aggregates.','A backfill reprocesses a defined input range with versioned logic and a controlled output strategy.',['Freeze input boundaries and code version.','Use isolated or idempotent outputs.','Reconcile results before cutover.'],'Backfills can saturate live dependencies or double-count shared outputs.','Why avoid blindly adding backfill results to live totals?','The same events may already be represented; use replacement, versioned outputs or deduplication.','A backfill needs a cutover contract.'],
 'pipeline-error-handling':['Keep poison data from blocking all progress.','One malformed event is retried forever while the backlog grows.','Separate transient infrastructure errors from invalid records and preserve enough context for safe reprocessing.',['Bound retries with backoff.','Quarantine invalid records with redacted diagnostics.','Define ownership and replay after correction.'],'Skipping records changes completeness and may violate ordering requirements.','Is a dead-letter queue a completed recovery?','No. Someone must monitor, diagnose and safely resolve or replay its contents.','Quarantine needs an operational owner.'],
 'materialized-views-streaming':['Store the answer you need to read quickly.','A dashboard repeatedly recomputes totals from a large event history.','A materialized view incrementally maintains query-oriented state from source changes.',['Define a stable update identity.','Handle duplicates, corrections and deletion.','Monitor lag and support rebuild.'],'Fast reads cost update work, storage and synchronization complexity.','How can replay double a total?','If updates are applied additively without recognizing events already represented in the view.','Derived views must be rebuildable and replay-safe.'],
 'time-series-patterns':['Model time, series identity and retention together.','High-cardinality metric labels overwhelm indexing and storage.','Time-series workloads organize timestamped observations by series and often downsample or expire old detail.',['Bound label cardinality.','Choose partition and retention windows.','Define late samples and aggregation rules.'],'Downsampling reduces cost but loses detail that cannot be recreated without raw data.','Why distinguish counters from gauges?','Their aggregation and interpretation differ; summing arbitrary gauge samples may not represent a meaningful total.','Preserve the semantics of each measurement.'],
 'streaming-analytics-architecture':['Separate ingestion, processing and serving contracts.','A dashboard serves partial results without exposing processing lag.','An analytics architecture connects durable input, transformation, state and query serving with explicit freshness and correctness policies.',['Trace event identity through the pipeline.','Set checkpoint and sink behavior.','Expose freshness, completeness and recovery metrics.'],'A low-latency processor cannot compensate for missing or incorrect upstream data.','What should the serving layer reveal?','The result version or freshness boundary and whether results remain provisional under the late-data policy.','Make data age visible to readers.'],
 'database-optimization':['Remove unnecessary work before distributing it.','A frequently executed query scans far more rows than it returns.','Plans, indexes, query shape and contention usually explain more than a database brand name.',['Measure slow and high-total-cost queries.','Inspect actual plans and cardinalities.','Validate changes against writes and representative data.'],'One faster query can make writes or other queries slower through added indexes.','Why test with production-like distributions?','Selectivity, skew and cache locality can change the optimizer’s plan and the true cost.','Optimize measured work under realistic conditions.'],
 'cache-always':['Make cache eligibility and fallback explicit.','A design routes every read through a cache without considering stale or private data.','A cache-first path returns a cached answer when valid, then loads authoritative data on a miss according to a defined policy.',['Specify which reads are eligible.','Define freshness and scope.','Protect the source on cache failure.'],'Cache-first behavior can hide stale data or amplify miss storms.','Should correctness-critical decisions always accept cached answers?','Only when their freshness and consistency contract is strong enough for that decision.','Fast access must preserve the required truth boundary.'],
 'pre-computing-pattern':['Spend work before the user asks.','An expensive report is calculated repeatedly for the same underlying data.','Precomputation shifts repeated read work to scheduled or change-driven updates of derived results.',['Identify reusable computations.','Choose update triggers and versioning.','Invalidate or rebuild when source logic changes.'],'Unused precomputed results waste resources and can become stale.','When does precomputation pay off?','When reuse and response-time benefits exceed update, storage and freshness costs.','Move computation only when its reuse is justified.'],
 'a-database-per-service':['Make one service authoritative for its data.','Two services write the same tables and break each other’s invariants.','Database-per-service isolates ownership; it can mean separate schemas or instances depending on isolation needs.',['Assign write ownership.','Expose data through contracts or events.','Plan cross-service queries and workflow recovery.'],'Ownership boundaries complicate joins and multi-service transactions.','Does this pattern require one physical server per service?','No. The key requirement is controlled ownership and access; physical isolation depends on operational needs.','Protect ownership before choosing deployment topology.'],
 'multi-system-data-sync':['Expect synchronization to fail between writes.','An order commits locally but its search index and event stream are not updated.','Choose an authoritative source and propagate durable change intent using an outbox or change feed, with replay-safe consumers.',['Write local state and intent atomically where possible.','Preserve stable change identity and position.','Reconcile lag, duplicates and missing updates.'],'Synchronization introduces temporary disagreement and recovery work.','Can sequential writes to three systems guarantee agreement?','No. A crash or timeout between writes can leave different systems at different states.','Own the source, log the intent, reconcile derived copies.'],
 'unique-id-generators':['Separate uniqueness from business intent.','Multiple writers generate colliding IDs or assume random IDs provide global time order.','Central sequences, UUIDs and time/worker-based IDs make different coordination, size and ordering trade-offs.',['Define uniqueness scope.','Evaluate clock and worker-ID assumptions.','Use database constraints as a final uniqueness boundary.'],'Time-based ordering can be affected by clock behavior; random IDs can affect index locality.','Is a newly generated UUID on every retry an idempotency strategy?','No. Retry attempts need the same logical operation identity, not merely individually unique IDs.','Unique attempts do not identify one intent.'],
 'rate-limiting-patterns':['Control admission before capacity is exhausted.','One tenant consumes most request capacity and affects everyone else.','Token buckets, fixed or sliding windows and concurrency limits govern different aspects of admission.',['Choose tenant and operation scope.','Specify distributed accounting behavior.','Return a documented rejection and retry policy.'],'Approximate distributed limits trade precision for availability and cost.','Does a QPS limit bound in-flight work?','Not by itself. Slow requests can accumulate; concurrency limits protect that dimension.','Limit rate and resource occupancy deliberately.'],
 'two-stage-processing':['Separate acceptance from expensive completion.','A request needs quick validation followed by long-running processing.','Stage one durably accepts a valid intent; stage two performs the work and records an observable outcome.',['Persist operation identity and status.','Transfer work durably.','Expose completion and recovery to the caller.'],'Fast acceptance adds asynchronous state and an eventual failure path.','What must stage one establish before responding accepted?','Enough durable state to recover and track the operation under the documented contract.','Acceptance is a promise to track work, not proof of completion.'],
 'fan-out-fan-in':['Bound parallel work and define completion.','A search request waits for results from many backends and one is very slow.','Fan-out starts independent work; fan-in combines results using an explicit deadline and completeness policy.',['Bound concurrency and total deadline.','Choose all, quorum or partial-result semantics.','Cancel unneeded work where possible.'],'Parallelism can increase downstream load and worsen tail sensitivity.','Why can more backends increase tail latency?','If completion waits for all responses, the slowest participant determines the finishing time.','Parallel work still needs a bounded aggregation contract.'],
 'system-design-template':['Use a checklist to expose decisions.','A discussion jumps into storage before identifying user actions and invariants.','A template organizes requirements, estimates, APIs, data, architecture and deep dives while leaving room for the specific problem.',['Clarify scope and quality targets.','Trace one end-to-end operation.','Test the most consequential failure and bottleneck.'],'A rigid template can waste time on irrelevant details.','What should drive the deepest discussion?','The operation or failure with the largest effect on the stated requirements and correctness rules.','Use structure to support reasoning.'],
 'system-design-template-social-media-comments':['Apply the sequence to a concrete feature.','A comment service must support posts, pagination, moderation and deletion at scale.','Model comments by post with stable IDs and deterministic ordering; separate authoritative writes from derived counts and notifications.',['Define create, list, delete and moderation contracts.','Estimate hot-post skew and choose pagination.','Walk duplicate creation, delayed moderation and count reconciliation.'],'Denormalized counts and feeds add synchronization lag and correction work.','How do you prevent duplicate comments after a client timeout?','Use a stable creation intent and a durable uniqueness boundary, then replay or query the original result.','A complete design follows one comment through its lifecycle.']
};
